{"name":"Pure Hedonist","hex":"#ff2255","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"pure","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Free of flaws or imperfections; unsullied.","Free of foreign material or pollutants."]},{"part":"Adverb","definitions":["to a great extent or degree; extremely; exceedingly."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/pure"},{"term":"hedonist","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Someone devoted to hedonism."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/hedonist"}],"entries":[{"title":"Hedonism","blocks":[{"text":"Hedonism is a family of philosophical views that prioritize pleasure. Psychological hedonism is the theory that all human behavior is motivated by the desire to maximize pleasure and minimize pain. As a form of egoism, it suggests that people only help others if they expect a personal benefit. Axiological hedonism is the view that pleasure is the sole source of intrinsic value. It asserts that other things, like knowledge and money, only have value insofar as they produce pleasure and reduce pain. This view divides into quantitative hedonism, which only considers the intensity and duration of pleasures, and qualitative hedonism, which identifies quality as another relevant factor. The closely related position of prudential hedonism states that pleasure and pain are the only factors of well-being. Ethical hedonism applies axiological hedonism to morality, arguing that people have a moral duty to pursue pleasure and avoid pain. Utilitarian versions assert that the goal is to increase overall happiness for everyone, whereas egoistic versions state that each person should only pursue their own pleasure. Outside the academic context, hedonism is sometimes used as a pejorative term for an egoistic lifestyle seeking short-term gratification."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c1/Role_of_pleasure_in_hedonism.svg/330px-Role_of_pleasure_in_hedonism.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hedonism"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d8/Thomas_Hobbes_%28portrait%29.jpg/500px-Thomas_Hobbes_%28portrait%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d8/Thomas_Hobbes_%28portrait%29.jpg/500px-Thomas_Hobbes_%28portrait%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Thomas Hobbes was a key advocate of psychological hedonism.[6]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AThomas_Hobbes_(portrait).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b2/Robert_Nozick_1977_Libertarian_Review_cover.jpg/500px-Robert_Nozick_1977_Libertarian_Review_cover.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b2/Robert_Nozick_1977_Libertarian_Review_cover.jpg/500px-Robert_Nozick_1977_Libertarian_Review_cover.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Robert Nozick criticized hedonism for not distinguishing between authentic and inauthentic pleasures.[21]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARobert_Nozick_1977_Libertarian_Review_cover.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/09/Epicurus-Altes_Museum.jpg/500px-Epicurus-Altes_Museum.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/09/Epicurus-Altes_Museum.jpg/1280px-Epicurus-Altes_Museum.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/09/Epicurus-Altes_Museum.jpg/500px-Epicurus-Altes_Museum.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Epicurus developed a nuanced form of ethical hedonism, arguing that a tranquil state of mind cultivated through moderation leads to the greatest overall happiness.[29]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AEpicurus-Altes_Museum.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/66/VanGogh-starry_night_ballance1.jpg/500px-VanGogh-starry_night_ballance1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/66/VanGogh-starry_night_ballance1.jpg/1280px-VanGogh-starry_night_ballance1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/66/VanGogh-starry_night_ballance1.jpg/500px-VanGogh-starry_night_ballance1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Aesthetic hedonism states that any entity, such as Vincent van Gogh's painting The Starry Night, is beautiful if it causes aesthetic pleasure.[37]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AVanGogh-starry_night_ballance1.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/25/Aristippus.jpg/330px-Aristippus.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/25/Aristippus.jpg/330px-Aristippus.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Aristippus of Cyrene is often seen as the first proponent of philosophical hedonism.[62]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAristippus.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c8/Jeremy_Bentham_by_Henry_William_Pickersgill_detail.jpg/500px-Jeremy_Bentham_by_Henry_William_Pickersgill_detail.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c8/Jeremy_Bentham_by_Henry_William_Pickersgill_detail.jpg/500px-Jeremy_Bentham_by_Henry_